From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: stop advancing the MIPS's NewCall ring after the CALL_ACK

SendComm2CPUEx did one thing after a successful CALL_ACK that no other
part of the driver does: it advanced the read index of a ring it does not
own.

    fifo_ItemRdNext((u32 *)(share_seq_w + 32));

share_seq_w here is getShareSeqW(1, 0), the ring at 0x4E3026B8 whose
local_cpu is the MIPS. Its FIFO at +32 is the MIPS's NewCall ring: the
MIPS ISR side pushes the pointer to our CALL slot there
(Comm_Add2NewCallFifo, display.bin 0x8b11d418) and then sends the
CALL_ACK; the MIPS BG thread pops it later (Comm_GetCallbyChannel,
0x8b11d81c, fifo_getItemRd @0x8b11dae0). One writer, one reader -- and we
were a second reader, racing the BG thread for the very entry we had just
sent.

When our rd++ landed first, the BG thread found the ring empty
(0x8b11dae8), returned NULL (0x8b11dfc4) and went back to sleep
(BG_Thread 0x8b123c0c). Nothing else ran for that call: no
Comm_ReleaseFreeCall (0x8b123cc0 -- hence one FreeCall slot lost per
lost answer, permanently), no handler, no RETURN, no elog line. When the
BG thread popped first, our rd++ hit an empty ring and was a no-op. That
is exactly the picture in doku/nachtlog/C3 and C4: ~30 % of dense calls
acknowledged but never answered, the outgoing ring leaking one slot each,
the incoming ring untouched, nothing in the msgbox.

It also explains the only knob that ever changed the loss. Our rd++ runs
the moment the ACK semaphore wakes us. tx_delay_post_us moves that moment
(100 us: 7/8 lost, 200: 4/40, 1000: 0/40); tx_delay_pre_us moves the
doorbell instead and changes nothing (11/20, 15/20); the three console
lines before the semaphore moved it by ~8 ms, which is why the console
was the accidental settle; the legacy daemon's CALL_GAP_MS and the
"FreeCall pool symptom" it throttled against were the same leak.

The comment justifying the line ("MIPS's update via KSEG0 cache never
propagates to ARM DDR view") does not hold: in every run with the console
on, our rd++ came milliseconds after the BG thread's and was a no-op, yet
500 (C2) and 40 (C3) calls went through without a single "FIFO nearly
full" from the step-7 check on the same ring. The MIPS drains its ring
itself, and we see it. The line had already been recorded as "no effect"
when it was tried (re/notes/DEAD-ENDS.md:228, HANDOFF-PQD-…-F.md:151) and
survived into 0014 with a new justification.

Replaced by a read-only observation: if the entry is still in the ring
when we wake, count it. That number, shown in watch as
"newcall N mal beim CALL_ACK noch ungelesen", is how many calls the old
line would have stolen. With dense calls and a silent console it has to
sit near the old loss rate while "ohne Antwort" stays at 0 -- if it does
not, this explanation is wrong and the patch must go.

Not compiled. Dry-run against the 77-patch series only. Analysis in
mainline/patches/vorschlaege/mips-return/BEFUND.md.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN inst-a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c inst-b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c	2026-09-07 17:23:04.975137106 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c	2026-09-07 17:26:44.140657797 +0200
@@ -526,6 +526,13 @@
 atomic_t cpu_comm_timeouts = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
 
 /*
+ * How often the MIPS had not yet taken our CALL out of its NewCall ring when
+ * the CALL_ACK woke us. Read-only observation in SendComm2CPUEx; every one of
+ * these is a call the removed fifo_ItemRdNext() would have stolen.
+ */
+atomic_t cpu_comm_newcall_pending_at_ack = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+
+/*
  * Measuring knobs, /sys/kernel/debug/cpu_comm/tx_delay_{pre,post}_us. Default
  * 0 = one comparison per call. See SendComm2CPUEx for what each one separates.
  */
@@ -1038,6 +1045,7 @@
 		       "rtable    version %u count %u\n"
 		       "spinlock2 owner=%u refcnt=%u thread=0x%08x %s\n"
 		       "calls_out %u, davon %u ohne Antwort\n"
+		       "newcall   %u mal beim CALL_ACK noch ungelesen\n"
 		       "freecall  rd=%u wr=%u cap=%u, belegt %u\n"
 		       "handlers  %u\n",
 		       atomic_read(&cpu_comm_rx_calls),
@@ -1049,6 +1057,7 @@
 		       lock.owner, lock.ref_count, lock.thread_id,
 		       cpu_comm_rt_lock_idle(&lock) ? "frei" : "BELEGT",
 		       atomic_read(&cpu_comm_calls_out), atomic_read(&cpu_comm_timeouts),
+		       atomic_read(&cpu_comm_newcall_pending_at_ack),
 		       fc_rd, fc_wr, fc_cap, fc_used,
 		       n_snap);
 
diff -ruN inst-a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h inst-b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h	2026-09-07 17:23:04.975272687 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h	2026-09-07 17:26:44.140734677 +0200
@@ -691,6 +691,7 @@
 
 extern atomic_t cpu_comm_calls_out;
 extern atomic_t cpu_comm_timeouts;
+extern atomic_t cpu_comm_newcall_pending_at_ack;
 extern u32 cpu_comm_tx_delay_pre_us;
 extern u32 cpu_comm_tx_delay_post_us;
 int  AddReturn2Fifo(u32 *fifo, void *data);
diff -ruN inst-a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c inst-b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c	2026-09-07 17:23:04.975353727 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c	2026-09-07 17:26:44.140343564 +0200
@@ -597,9 +597,44 @@
 			ReleaseWaitComm(dst_cpu, (unsigned long)wait_ptr);
 		}
 	} else {
-		/* Advance CMD-FIFO Rd on ARM side — MIPS's update via KSEG0 cache
-		 * never propagates to ARM DDR view. Without this: -EBUSY at 19. */
-		fifo_ItemRdNext((u32 *)(share_seq_w + 32));
+		/*
+		 * Kein fifo_ItemRdNext() auf share_seq_w + 32 mehr.
+		 *
+		 * Diese FIFO ist die NewCall-FIFO des MIPS, nicht unsere. Seine
+		 * ISR-Seite legt den Eintrag dort ab (Comm_Add2NewCallFifo,
+		 * display.bin 0x8b11d418: fifo_getItemWr @0x8b11d478, Eintrag
+		 * @0x8b11d48c, fifo_requestItemWr @0x8b11d488), sein BG-Thread
+		 * holt ihn wieder heraus (Comm_GetCallbyChannel 0x8b11d81c:
+		 * fifo_getItemRd @0x8b11dae0, fifo_ItemRdNext @0x8b11ddc0). Ein
+		 * Ring mit einem Schreiber und einem Leser -- und wir waren der
+		 * zweite Leser. Kam unser rd++ vor dem des BG-Threads, sah der
+		 * den Ring leer (0x8b11dae8), gab NULL zurueck (0x8b11dfc4) und
+		 * legte sich wieder schlafen (BG_Thread 0x8b123c0c). Der Ruf war
+		 * quittiert, aber nie bearbeitet: kein Comm_ReleaseFreeCall
+		 * (0x8b123cc0, deshalb der dauerhaft belegte Slot), kein Handler,
+		 * kein RETURN, kein elog. Kam der BG-Thread zuerst, war unser
+		 * rd++ ein Leerlauf (rd == wr). Genau das Bild aus
+		 * doku/nachtlog/C3 und C4 -- und der Zeitpunkt unseres rd++ ist
+		 * das Aufwachen aus der ACK-Semaphore, also genau die Groesse, die
+		 * tx_delay_post_us, die Konsolenzeilen und der Settle des
+		 * Legacy-Daemons verschoben haben.
+		 *
+		 * Die alte Begruendung ("MIPS's update via KSEG0 cache never
+		 * propagates") traegt nicht: in jedem Lauf mit eingeschalteter
+		 * Konsole kam unser rd++ Millisekunden nach dem des BG-Threads
+		 * und war ein Leerlauf, und die Rufe liefen trotzdem ohne "FIFO
+		 * nearly full" durch (C2: 500/500, C3: 40/40). Den Ring raeumt der
+		 * MIPS selbst, fuer uns sichtbar.
+		 *
+		 * Was bleibt, ist ein Blick ohne Schreibzugriff: liegt der Eintrag
+		 * in diesem Moment noch im Ring, haette die alte Zeile ihn
+		 * gestohlen. Der Zaehler steht in watch und muss bei dichten
+		 * Rufen ungefaehr die fruehere Verlustrate zeigen, waehrend
+		 * "ohne Antwort" bei 0 bleibt -- sonst stimmt diese Erklaerung
+		 * nicht. mainline/patches/vorschlaege/mips-return/BEFUND.md.
+		 */
+		if (fifo_getCount((u32 *)(share_seq_w + 32)) > 0)
+			atomic_inc(&cpu_comm_newcall_pending_at_ack);
 	}
 
 out_cleanup_wait:
